    "Notes Updated": "6/20/2024",
    "Scouting Notes": "He can really pick it at shortstop\n Despite being a tall guy, he moves well, has a good glove, strong/plus arm\n Hit tool is really strong, good bat to ball, could use work on the swing decisions\n Power is in there, but not showing in games super consistently yet\n Will be important to add that power eventually if he's to reach his ceiling\n Speed is closer to average than plus, but he's a very good baserunner\n Could be a long term SS for the Brewers, probably a T100 guy in the game currently",
    "Notes Updated 2": "7/12/2023",
    "Scouting Notes 2": "2023 Post Draft\nWhen the Brewers made this choice I actually jumped up out of my seat. By this point in the draft, I was actually thinking they were already running low on slot bonus money to spend. Frankly, I had written off Pratt as a possibility long before this sixth round selection. Assuming they do sign him, this is probably the biggest steal of the entire draft. At 6’5, many scouts believe he will be able to stick at shortstop. Based on the little video of his defense available, I am inclined to agree. For a player his size, he moves extremely well on the dirt and his arm is quick enough to handle short as well. Offensively he is currently hit over power, but considering his frame, there is a lot of room to grow in the power department. Even if the power never does develop, there is a lot to like in his profile. According to Baseball America, “Over a 668  pitch sample, Pratt has just a 12% overall miss rate and 17% chase rate. On pitches in the zone, his miss rate falls to just 6%.” Those are fantastic numbers for a player his age. He has a bit of a leg kick, but an extremely smooth looking swing that allows him to hit with some authority to all fields. It would not shock me if later on down the road, Pratt ended up being the best player the Brewers selected in this draft class. The upside is similar to that of Gunnar Henderson.",
